course: dictionary
lesson: 10069
title: Than: comparing two things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Use «than» when you compare two things. You say one is bigger, faster or better than the other. «Breathed-out air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than breathed-in air.» The sentence compares two kinds of air, and «than» joins the two halves of the comparison.
explain: Look for the pattern: a comparing word like «more», «less», «fewer», «older» or «bigger», then «than». «Listening kindly is better than saying "you told me".» Here «than» links the better choice with the one it beats.
explain: «Than» also comes after «rather» and «other»: «rather walk than ride» and «anyone other than Ben». In «No sooner had we left than it rained», the words «no sooner … than» mean it rained just after we left.
explain: People often say «than me», as in «He's taller than me». In formal writing you may see «than I». Ask your teacher which one to use in tests.
fact: «Than» is used to compare two things.
fact: Comparing words such as «more», «less», «fewer», «older» and «bigger» often come just before «than».
fact: «No sooner … than» means just after: «No sooner had we left than it rained».
fact: In maths, the signs > and < are read «greater than» and «less than».
fact: «Vent water can be hotter than three hundred degrees, but it does not boil» because of the huge pressure of the deep sea.
exercise: «Breathed-out air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than breathed-in air.» Which two things are being compared? | *Breathed-out air and breathed-in air | Carbon dioxide and breathing | Oxygen and carbon dioxide | Oxygen and air
exercise: «No sooner had we left than it rained.» What does this mean? | It rained before we left | We left because it rained every day | *It started raining just after we left | It was raining, so we stayed inside
exercise: Fill the gap: «The sloth can swim about three times faster ___ it climbs.» | thin | there | then | *than
test: Fill the gap: «Listening kindly is ___ saying "you told me".» | *better than | better that | bitter than | better then
test: «Rather walk than ride.» What does this suggest? | Walk and ride at the same time | *The speaker would choose walking over riding | Walking is always slow and tiring | Riding is always faster and better
exam: In maths, which sign means «greater than»? | < | *> | ≠ | =
